{"title":"Longest Palindromic Substring","difficulty":"Medium","statement":"Given a string `s`, find its **longest palindromic substring**: the longest contiguous block of characters that reads the same forwards and backwards.\n\nIf several palindromic substrings share the maximum length, print the one that **starts at the smallest index** in `s`. A single character is a palindrome, so an answer always exists.\n\nPrint the substring itself.\n\n**Follow-up:** write working code first, then optimise. Can you go below the `O(n^2)` of expanding around every centre, for example with Manacher's algorithm in `O(n)`?","inputFormat":"A single line containing the string `s`.","outputFormat":"Print the longest palindromic substring (the leftmost one if there is a tie).","constraints":["1 ≤ |s| ≤ 2000","`s` consists of lowercase English letters only"],"examples":[{"input":"forgeeksskeegfor","output":"geeksskeeg","explanation":"`geeksskeeg` reads the same in both directions and has length 10; no longer palindrome exists in the string."},{"input":"xabacdc","output":"aba","explanation":"`aba` (starting at index 1) and `cdc` (starting at index 4) both have length 3. The leftmost one is printed."},{"input":"abcd","output":"a","explanation":"No two adjacent characters match, so every palindrome has length 1 and the leftmost one, `a`, is the answer."}],"tests":[{"input":"a","output":"a"},{"input":"ab","output":"a"},{"input":"aa","output":"aa"},{"input":"abc","output":"a"},{"input":"aba","output":"aba"},{"input":"abba","output":"abba"},{"input":"cbbd","output":"bb"},{"input":"aaaa","output":"aaaa"},{"input":"abacabad","output":"abacaba"},{"input":"zzabzz","output":"zz"},{"input":"xukgpbuzpcebkqgchncrhuvqg","output":"x"},{"input":"aaabb","output":"aaa"},{"input":"bdacbaabdbdcbc","output":"baab"},{"input":"aabaacabcaaccbcccbc","output":"cbcccbc"},{"input":"bbacc","output":"bb"},{"input":"dcdddd","output":"dddd"},{"input":"aacaaccc","output":"aacaa"},{"input":"baaaccabcbcabaccbb","output"